{{smaller block|<poem>That liked me ''wonders'' wele.</poem>
{{right|''Rom. of the Rose'', l. 27.}}}}
{{smaller block|[This is the reading of the original edition and of the manuscripts. It has been changed into ''wonderous'' in some modern editions, and perhaps correctly.]}}

'''wondersly†''', ''adv.'' [< ''wonders'' + ''-ly''<sup>2</sup>.] Wonderfully.
{{smaller block|Where suche a solempne yerely myracle is wrought so ''wondersly'' in the face of the world.
{{right|''Sir T. More'', Works, p. 134.}}}}

'''wonder-stone''' (wun′dėr-stōn), ''n.'' The name given to a bed occurring in the Red Marl (Triassic) near Wells, England, which is described by Buckland and Conybeare as being “a beautiful breccia, consisting of yellow transparent crystals of carbonate of lime disseminated through a dark red earthy dolomite.”

'''wonderstricken, wonderstruck''' (wun′dėr-strik″n, wun′dėr-struk), ''a.'' Struck with wonder, admiration, or surprise.
{{smaller block|<poem>Ascanius, ''wonder-struck'' to see
That image of his filial piety.</poem>
{{right|''Dryden'', Æneid, ix. 394.}}
<poem>Cast his strong arms about his drooping wife,
And kiss’d his ''wonder-stricken'' little ones.</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', Enoch Arden.}}}}

'''wonder-wonder''' (wun′dėr-wun″dėr), ''n.'' See ''Rafflesia''.

'''wonderwork''' (wun′dėr-wėrk), ''n.'' [< ME. ''wonderworc'', < AS. ''wundorweorc'' (Stratmann) (= G. ''wunderwerk''); as ''wonder'' + ''work'', ''n.''] A won-

readings.c2s1:
derful work or act; a prodigy; a miracle; thaumaturgy.
{{smaller block|<poem>Such as in strange land
He found in ''wonder-works'' of God and Nature’s hand.</poem>
{{right|''Byron'', Childe Harold, iii. 10.}}}}

'''wonderworker''' (wun′dėr-wėr″kėr), ''n.'' One who performs wonders or surprising things; a thaumaturgist. ''I. D’Israeli'', Curios. of Lit., II. 162.

'''wonderworking''' (wun′dėr-wėr″king), ''a.'' Doing wonders or surprising things. ''G. Herbert'', Country Parson, xxxii.

'''wonder-wounded''' (wun′dėr-wön″ded), ''a.'' Struck with wonder or surprise; wonder-stricken.
{{smaller block|<poem>What is he whose grief . . .
Conjures the wandering stars, and makes them stand
Like ''wonder-wounded'' hearers? ''Shak.'', Hamlet, v. 1. 280.</poem>}}

'''wondrous''' (wun′drus), ''a.'' [Formerly ''wonderous'', ''wonderouse'', < ''wonder'' + ''-ous''; prob. suggested by ''marvelous'', etc., but in part a substitute for early mod. E. ''wonders'': see ''wonders''.] '''I.''' ''a.'' Of a kind or degree to excite wonder; wonderful; marvelous; strange.
{{smaller block|That I may publish with the voice of thanksgiving, and tell of all thy ''wondrous'' works. Ps. xxvi. 7.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>Wherefore gaze this goodly company,
As if they saw some ''wondrous'' monument?</poem>
{{right|''Shak.'', T. of the S., iii. 2. 97.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>And yet no Angel envy’d Him his place
Who ever look’d upon his ''wonderous'' face.</poem>
{{right|''J. Beaumont'', Psyche, ii. 214.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>''Wondrous'' truths, and manifold as ''wondrous'',
God hath written in those stars above.</poem>}}

readings.c3s1:
pp. of ''won''<sup>1</sup>: see ''won''<sup>1</sup>.] '''I.''' ''intrans.'' '''1.''' To be accustomed or habituated; use; be used.
{{smaller block|When soon the goodly Wyre, that ''wonted'' was so high<br>Her stately top to rear, . . .<br>Of Erisicthon’s end begins her to bethink.
{{right|''Drayton'', Polyolbion, vii. 256.}}
The jessamine that round the straw-roof’d cot<br>Its fragrant branches wreathed, beneath whose shade<br>I ''wont'' to sit and watch the setting sun<br>And hear the thrush’s song. {{right|''Southey.''}}}}
'''2.''' To dwell; make one’s home.
{{smaller block|The king’s fisher ''wonts'' commonly by the waterside and nestles in hollow banks. {{right|''Sir R. L’Estrange.''}}}}

'''II.''' ''trans.'' To accustom; habituate.
{{smaller block|These, that in youth have ''wonted'' themselves to the load of less sins, want not increase of strength according to the increase of their burdens. ''Rev. T. Adams'', Works, I. 354.}}

'''wont'''<sup>1</sup> (wunt), ''n.'' [< ''wont''<sup>1</sup>, ''a.'' and ''v.'' Cf. ''won''<sup>1</sup>, ''wone'', ''n.''] Custom; habit; practice; way.
{{smaller block|’Tis not his ''wont'' to be the hindmost man.
{{right|''Shak.'', 2 Hen. VI., iii. 1. 2.}}
Rather than I wou’d break my old ''Wont.''
{{right|''Etherege'', She Would if She Could, v. i.}}
The heart grows hardened with perpetual ''wont.''
{{right|''Lowell'', Parting of the Ways.}}}}
'''Use and wont.''' {{smaller|See ''use''<sup>1</sup>.}}

'''wont'''<sup>2</sup>†, ''v.'' An obsolete form of ''want''<sup>1</sup>.
{{smaller block|<poem>Make
For hem, yf other water ''wonte'', a lake.</poem>
{{right|''Palladius'', Husbondrie (E. E. T. S.), p. 26.}}}}

'''wont'''<sup>3</sup>, ''n.'' A variant of ''want''<sup>2</sup>.

'''won’t''' (wunt or wōnt). A contraction of ''woll not''—that is, ''will not.''

'''wonted''' (wun′ted), ''p. a.'' [< ''wont''<sup>1</sup> + ''-ed''<sup>2</sup>.] '''1.''' Accustomed; made or having become familiar by using, frequenting, etc.

readings.c3s2:
{{smaller block|<poem>The stately lord, which ''woonted'' was to kepe
A court at home, is now come vp to courte.</poem>
{{right|''Gascoigne'', Steele Glas (ed. Arber), p. 62.}}}}
{{smaller block|Hepzibah had fully satisfied herself of the impossibility of ever becoming ''wonted'' to this peevishly obstreperous little [shop-]bell. {{right|''Hawthorne'', Seven Gables, v.}}}}
'''2.''' Customary or familiar by being used, done, frequented, enjoined, experienced, or the like; usual.
{{smaller block|<poem>She did her ''wonted'' course forslowe.</poem>
{{right|''Spenser'', F. Q., VII. vi. 16.}}}}
{{smaller block|To pay our ''wonted'' tribute. ''Shak.'', Cymbeline, v. 5. 462.}}
{{smaller block|<poem>To this the courteous Prince
Accorded with his ''wonted'' courtesy.</poem>
{{right|''Tennyson'', Lancelot and Elaine.}}}}

'''wontedness''' (wun′ted-nes), ''n.'' The state of being wonted or accustomed; customariness.
{{smaller block|''Wontedness'' of opinion. {{right|''Eikon Basilike'', p. 163.}}}}

'''wontless''' (wunt′les), ''a.'' [< ''wont''<sup>1</sup> + ''-less''.] Unaccustomed; unused. [Rare.]
{{smaller block|<poem>What ''wontlesse'' fury dost thou now inspire
Into my feeble breast, too full of thee?</poem>
{{right|''Spenser'', In Honour of Beautie, l. 2.}}}}
{{smaller block|<poem>He, remembering the past day
When from his name the affrighted sons of France
Fled trembling, all astonished at their force
And ''wontless'' valour, rages round the field
Dreadful in anger.</poem>
{{right|''Southey''.}}}}

